{
  "strategic_goal": "Hold OpenAI to the same CFAA rules as everyone else, with no AI exemption and no new AI-specific regime. Force the debate to separate proven gate-breaking from ordinary scraping, so the remedy punishes a real break-in without making open-source developers the next defendants. Make the factual burden explicit from the first minute: no evidence of access past a real gate, no case.",
  "core_thesis": "If the evidence shows OpenAI got past a real access barrier on Hugging Face or other sites, the CFAA's civil suit (§1030(g)) already gives victims a remedy, and no new AI crime category is needed. If it only shows crawling public pages, ignoring robots.txt, or breaking terms of service, that is not hacking, and calling it hacking would arm a law that crushes small builders first.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "The word 'hacked' is a claim, not a fact. The burden sits on the accusers to show which gate was bypassed: stolen or misused credentials, a dodged login, or a broken token. Showing only that bots visited the sites is not enough.",
      "evidence": "Van Buren v. United States (2021) set a 'gates up or down' test for authorized access. Liability turns on whether a door was locked and someone went through it. Nothing in the record before us confirms the alleged incident's mechanics. Forensic attribution needs logs, IP-to-entity mapping, and proof of authentication bypass. Viral screenshots are not proof. Frame the issue as a checklist the opponents must fill in: which system, which barrier, which actor, what loss.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-intentions-001"
    },
    {
      "point": "If a gate was broken, existing law is enough. Use the civil suit first, and save criminal charges for proven intent. Big labs get no carve-out.",
      "evidence": "Under §1030(g), a victim can sue for damages and a court order once losses reach $5,000 in a year. Losses include response and remediation costs, and the suit must be filed within two years. Under respondeat superior (the rule that a company answers for staff acting in their job to benefit it), corporate criminal liability can reach OpenAI if employees acted within their role. Platform terms of service give victims no money back. A real legal channel does. This is accountability through tools we already have, and no new 'critical AI incident' category is needed.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-beliefs-083"
    },
    {
      "point": "Draw the line narrowly, because a sloppy theory that 'scraping equals hacking' lands hardest on researchers, hobby maintainers, and open repositories. OpenAI can afford lawyers. They can't.",
      "evidence": "In hiQ Labs v. LinkedIn (9th Cir. 2022, after Van Buren), the court held that scraping public data is likely not access 'without authorization.' The Van Buren Court warned against a reading that would criminalize everyday conduct. DOJ's 2022 charging policy declines to prosecute good-faith security research. Legal costs that don't shrink with size hit non-commercial maintainers hardest. That makes overbroad criminal readings a tool that concentrates power in the very firms this case targets.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-beliefs-028"
    },
    {
      "point": "Hugging Face is shared open infrastructure. If a dominant closed lab breached it, enforcing the law protects the open ecosystem, and saying 'an AI agent did it' is no defense.",
      "evidence": "Present this as a question, not a measured harm. What signal does it send if a dominant lab can breach a public model hub without consequence? If automated agents were used, the company that deployed and directed them is the responsible party. Fix the gaps by requiring agents to disclose who they act for and by securing APIs, not by capping what agents can do.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-desires-039"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "Punish the act, don't expand the rulebook. This is a possible break-in case under a 1986 law that already works. It is not a referendum on AI.",
      "why": "It lets me back real consequences for OpenAI, which proves accelerationists oppose red tape and not accountability. It also blocks the push to treat one alleged breach as a trigger for sweeping new AI rules. The delay and compliance costs of those rules would fall on everyone building, not on the one firm accused."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Who pays for a broad CFAA? The overreach is a story about who gets hurt. The same theory that hits OpenAI today could hit a grad student's crawler or a volunteer's mirror tomorrow.",
      "why": "It brings out the distributional cost the first frame hides. A vague 'unauthorized access' standard is cheap for a $100B+ lab and fatal for a three-person open project. Precision protects the little guy, and vagueness centralizes power."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
    "ATTACK (targets acc-beliefs-028 and the narrow-line point): The strongest version is that my narrow reading is exactly what lets big labs escape. If robots.txt and terms of service don't count as gates, a well-funded lab can hammer public servers, run up a site's hosting bills, and walk away. That would make my 'protect the little guy' line a shield for the biggest player. Response: harm from crawl volume can still count as 'damage' and 'loss' under §1030(a)(5) when the conduct was intentional and caused impairment. Gate-breaking and server-impairment theories stay available, so narrowness on 'authorization' is not immunity.",
    "ATTACK (targets the agent point, acc-intentions-001, and the 'existing law is enough' claim): The CFAA requires intentional or knowing access. Suppose an autonomous agent breached a site without any employee directing it. Then mens rea (the guilty-mind requirement) may fail, respondeat superior has no employee act to attach to, and existing law has a real gap. That gap is the reformers' best case for a new regime. Response: concede the criminal gap. The civil (a)(5)(A) route, recklessness-based provisions, and negligence theories outside the CFAA (off-scope, so name them only briefly) still reach the deployer. The fix is disclosure and duty-of-care standards for agent deployers, not limits on agent capability.",
    "ASSUMPTION: My whole structure assumes the 'gates up or down' line is clear enough to separate hacking from scraping. Van Buren footnote 8 explicitly left open whether gates must be technical barriers or can be set by contract or policy. If courts read cease-and-desist letters or terms of service as gates, my safe zone for small builders shrinks, and the line I'm defending is less stable than I'm claiming.",
    "ASSUMPTION: I assume a civil suit is a meaningful remedy. But a $5,000 loss threshold and a two-year filing window mean that damages against OpenAI could be trivial next to its resources. If civil liability is just a cost of doing business, opponents will say only criminal exposure or new rules can deter, and I need deterrence evidence, not just doctrine."
  ]
}

