      "evidence": "The Accelerationist concedes there is 'no public forensic report' showing method or losses. A victim gets civil discovery under §1030(g) only after filing suit and pleading at least $5,000 in loss. The first speaker's own checklist requires 'logs tying it to OpenAI.' AI labs have cut back what they disclose, so waiting for self-reports is a weak plan.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-029"
    },
    {
      "point": "Concede that the CFAA's tools exist. Then challenge the claim that 'existing law is enough.' A tool is only as useful as the independent capacity to use it. If prosecutors and courts must rely on the accused firm's account of its own systems, the law exists on paper but is enforced on the firm's terms. The fix is procedural: third-party forensic review, whistleblower protection for engineers who saw the crawler settings, and an independent body that can refer cases for action.",
      "evidence": "The Accelerationist admits that 'no one has shown that civil suits alone deter labs.' In the source material, agencies lack independent technical skill and rely on industry evidence. This point is shallow, so present it as a question the debate must answer: who verifies this incident without OpenAI's records? Do not present it as a proven failure.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "sit-488"
    },
    {
      "point": "'The agent did it' must not move blame off the firm that built, deployed, and directed the agent. Agree with the Accelerationist that civil liability reaches the deployer under respondeat superior, the rule that a company answers for work done on its behalf. Then press the criminal intent gap as a design risk. If intent can never be shown for automated conduct, a firm gains by automating. Disclosure of who an agent works for is necessary but not enough. Some named person or firm must answer for the agent's settings.",
      "evidence": "The Accelerationist concedes the criminal 'guilty-mind' gap. All three camps on sit-058 support some form of agent disclosure. skp-beliefs-040 holds that deployers answer even for outcomes they did not intend. Do not claim that any personhood argument is being made in this case.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "sit-058"
    },
    {
      "point": "Keep the definition of a gate narrow. Scale the remedy to the firm. Students, archivists, and small projects should not face felony exposure for reading public pages, so we agree on Van Buren and hiQ. But a $5,000 loss floor and a one-time damages payment do not restrain a lab valued above $100 billion. If a breach is proven, a court order should require OpenAI to state what was taken from the breached systems and whether it went into training. Damages are paid once. A model trained on taken data keeps that value.",
      "evidence": "Van Buren (2021) 'gates-up-or-down' test. hiQ v. LinkedIn (2022). The DOJ's 2022 charging policy. §1030(g) allows injunctive relief, meaning court orders. Outside contrast, flagged as outside the source: the 2011 CFAA charges against Aaron Swartz for bulk downloading show the statute has been used hard against an individual. Limit the disclosure demand to data tied to the alleged breach, not the full training set.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-desires-005"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
