- [skp-beliefs-041] AI Bias and Harm Are Well-Documented, Not Hypothetical: Cherry-picking (confirmation bias in evidence selection) — The node's framing compiles evidence of negative impacts and biases by design, which risks systematically underweighting the large volume of AI applications where bias audits reveal acceptable performance or net-positive outcomes, creating an incomplete evidentiary picture.
- [skp-beliefs-129] Safety Rhetoric as Infrastructure Cartel Maintenance: Genetic fallacy — The argument risks dismissing existential risk concerns entirely on the basis of who funds or promotes them, rather than engaging with the arguments on their merits. Even if some proponents benefit commercially, that does not establish the falsity of their safety claims.
- [skp-beliefs-141] AI Personhood as a Corporate Liability Shield: Slippery Slope — The node assumes that any movement toward AI legal personhood will inevitably be exploited for liability evasion, without adequately accounting for countervailing legal safeguards, judicial interpretation, or regulatory design that could prevent such misuse.
- [skp-beliefs-045] AI-Generated Content Causes Direct, Documented Harm to Individuals: Instrument/Agent Conflation — The claim frames AI as a direct causal agent of harm, when the proximate cause is malicious human intent; AI lowers friction but is not itself the harm-producing force, which risks misdirecting regulatory responses toward the tool rather than perpetrators.
- [skp-beliefs-005] Training Data Memorization as Structural Privacy Failure: Overgeneralization — Empirical memorization studies (e.g., Carlini et al.) are conducted on specific model families and sizes; the claim extends these findings to all LLMs as a categorical architectural property, ignoring variance across training regimes and model classes.
- [skp-desires-005] Distributed AI Power and Democratic Infrastructure: Nirvana fallacy — The node contrasts the flawed reality of concentrated corporate AI power against an idealized vision of democratic oversight and distributed control, without adequately accounting for the practical failures, inefficiencies, or corruption risks of the proposed alternative governance structures.
- [skp-desires-078] Foundational AI as a Public Good: Nirvana Fallacy — The desire implicitly contrasts an idealized public-good model against the failures of market concentration, without rigorously accounting for the well-documented failures of centralized or federated public infrastructure at scale.
- [skp-desires-076] Public Right to Know What AI Was Trained On: Wishful thinking / feasibility assumption — The desire presupposes that accurate, comprehensive identification of copyrighted works in massive training corpora is practically achievable, when current tooling and metadata availability make this extremely difficult at scale — conflating the normative goal with an assumed technical capability.
- [skp-intentions-014] Enforce Training Data Provenance Disclosure and Creator Compensation: Assumed efficacy — The node assumes that mandated disclosure will lead to better copyright compliance and accountability, but disclosure requirements alone do not guarantee enforcement or meaningful remediation — compliance theatre is a well-documented risk in regulatory contexts.
- [skp-intentions-136] Pinning AI Safety Liability to Commercial Infrastructure Executives: Single Point Accountability Fallacy — The node assumes that assigning a named individual resolves diffuse organizational responsibility, but for complex multi-team AI releases, no single individual can genuinely comprehend or control all risk vectors, making the accountability assignment nominally meaningful but practically hollow.

